#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093309} :activity/id #uuid "59e47f38-9dc9-538d-993c-2e3ee834c641" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050779 :topic/id #uuid "66051139-bfd1-53ce-b0e2-5c7d415815e5" :topic/math-academy-id 999} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093361 :tutorial/content "To calculate the arc length of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ between the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the arc length of the polar curve $r = 2,$ where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ The required arc length is shown in blue in the picture below.\n\n![](images/e9/e91cd717469db56426e39e8975aa04710ff2357c7a0d5de9d20f99bc5edcbb1c.png)\n\nWe will use the formula to compute the arc length. Since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 0,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the arc length can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}2\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46d71df2-8000-5586-bb24-748b23a02ce3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1141 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc4e82f8-a2bd-59bb-ab26-acf1b38e6eba" :step/math-academy-id 617629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092373 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092125 :question/id #uuid "8b8e3c66-fc60-57ac-baf7-0661e213d01e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7400"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050277}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446933}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "fb73f248-2c4e-50c5-b636-329ca36abf27" :step/math-academy-id 617630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092374 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092093 :question/id #uuid "57034318-5a6e-53f6-a8d3-ae8c61692947" :question/math-academy-id "e-641"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a533e8-9a7c-58f3-9d3c-7c08b3c6bfd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446935}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "4d753f5d-a62d-581e-9242-d4c1c4d4bef5" :step/math-academy-id 617633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093364 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula to compute the arc length bounded by a polar curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ r^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} \\right)^2} \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's start with the familiar formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nA polar curve is really just a parametric curve with parameter $\\theta$ and parametric equations given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the arc length formula for a parametric function with parameter $\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $x = r \\cos \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since $y=r\\sin \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $L,$ we reach the desired formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1821e80d-8c1b-5d2f-88dd-c207d2c3d99f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1144 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the Main Formula"} :step/id #uuid "a2419f97-10bb-5949-b766-7c403f31ea78" :step/math-academy-id 617636}] :activity/title "The Arc Length of a Polar Curve" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092470}]}
